<abstract>
<para>
&kappname; (<command>tdepackagekit</command>) installs, removes and updates software on the
Trinity Desktop Environment. It works through PackageKit, so it uses the same package database
as <command>apt</command> and the other package tools on your system.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>TDE</keyword>
<keyword>Trinity</keyword>
<keyword>PackageKit</keyword>
<keyword>tdepackagekit</keyword>
<keyword>software</keyword>
<keyword>packages</keyword>
<keyword>updates</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
&kappname; is a package manager for Trinity. In one window you can search for software, install
and remove it, review and install updates, see what has changed on your system, and turn software
repositories on or off. A tray icon, the update notifier, tells you when updates are available.
</para>

<para>
Nothing changes on your system until you press <guibutton>Apply</guibutton>. You can tick several
packages first, then apply them together. Before anything happens you see every package that
will be installed or removed as a result.
</para>

<para>
Open it from the Trinity menu under <guimenu>System</guimenu>, <guimenuitem>Software
Management</guimenuitem>, or run <command>tdepackagekit</command>.
</para>

<warning>
<para>
This program was written by an AI under human direction, and its code has not been reviewed line
by line. It changes the software installed on your computer, so keep backups.
</para>
</warning>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Browse page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="browse-details.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Browse page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</chapter>

<chapter id="using">
<title>Using &kappname;</title>

<para>
The sidebar on the left switches between four pages: <guilabel>Browse</guilabel>,
<guilabel>Updates</guilabel>, <guilabel>History</guilabel> and <guilabel>Repositories</guilabel>.
You can also press <keycombo action="simul"><keycap>Ctrl</keycap><keycap>1</keycap></keycombo>
to <keycombo action="simul"><keycap>Ctrl</keycap><keycap>4</keycap></keycombo>.
The bar at the bottom of the window shows your pending changes, with the
<guibutton>Apply</guibutton> and <guibutton>Clear</guibutton> buttons.
</para>

<sect1 id="browse">
<title>Finding and installing software</title>

<para>
Type in the search box and press <keycap>Enter</keycap>, or click a category on the left. The
list next to the search box chooses what to search: package names, descriptions, or file names.
</para>

<para>
The filters below the search box narrow the results:
</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Show</guilabel>: all packages, installed packages only, or available
packages only.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Graphical applications only</guilabel> hides libraries and
command-line tools.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Newest versions only</guilabel> shows one version of each
package.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>
A filter is hidden if your package system doesn't support it.
</para>

<para>
Select a package to see its details below the list. The tabs show its description, its files,
what it depends on, and what requires it.
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Tick a package to install it.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Untick an installed package to remove it.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
Ticking only queues the change. Keep browsing and tick more, then press
<guibutton>Apply</guibutton>. <guibutton>Clear</guibutton> drops every pending change.
</para>

<sect2 id="app-icons">
<title>Application icons</title>

<para>
The package lists can show each application's icon. The icons come from the AppStream data that
Debian publishes for its repositories, and apt only downloads them if one of these packages is
installed:
</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><literal>apt-config-icons</literal>: icons at 48 and 64 pixels.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><literal>apt-config-icons-large</literal>: also 128 pixel icons.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>
Install one, then refresh the package information with <guimenuitem>Check for Updates</guimenuitem>
(or <userinput><command>sudo apt update</command></userinput>). The icons appear the next time
you start &kappname;. Packages that aren't applications, such as libraries, have no icon of their
own and show a generic package icon. Without these packages, the lists show no icons.
</para>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="applying">
<title>Applying changes</title>

<para>
Pressing <guibutton>Apply</guibutton> first works out what will happen, without changing
anything. If other packages are affected, if anything will be removed, or if you are mixing
installs and updates, you are asked to confirm:
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Confirm Changes dialog</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="confirm-changes.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Confirm Changes dialog</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The changes then run in a progress window. <guibutton>Cancel</guibutton> works while the package
system allows it.
</para>

<para>
You may be asked for your password. That prompt comes from the Trinity authentication agent, in
the <literal>polkit-agent-tde</literal> package. Without a running agent, changes fail with a
<quote>not authorized</quote> error.
</para>

<para>
Depending on the packages, you may also be asked:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>License Agreement</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The package has terms you must accept. <guibutton>Continue</guibutton> stays
disabled until you tick the box.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Trust This Software Source?</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The package is signed with a key your system doesn't know. Check the fingerprint
with the publisher before you trust it. Pressing <keycap>Enter</keycap> cancels.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Unverified Software</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Nothing can confirm where the package came from. Every local
<filename>.deb</filename> file shows this. Install it only if you trust its source.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Restart Required</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Log out, or restart the computer, to finish the update.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
If something fails, the changes that didn't happen stay pending, so you can try again. See
<xref linkend="errors"/>.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="updates">
<title>Updates</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Updates page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="updates-details.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Updates page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Updates are grouped by severity, with critical and security updates first. Select an update to
see what it changes, its changelog and any security advisories.
</para>

<para>
The first time you open this page in a session, every update is ticked, so
<guibutton>Apply</guibutton> installs them all. Untick any you want to skip, or press
<guibutton>Select None</guibutton>. Blocked updates, which need something else to change first,
can't be ticked.
</para>

<para>
<guibutton>Check for Updates</guibutton> (also in the <guimenu>File</guimenu> menu, and
<keycap>F5</keycap>) downloads fresh package information. The sidebar shows how many updates are
waiting.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="history">
<title>History</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The History page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="history.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The History page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Lists what was installed, removed and updated, when, and whether it worked. Select a row to see
its packages. The list includes changes made with other PackageKit tools.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="repositories">
<title>Repositories</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Repositories page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="repositories.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Repositories page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Untick a repository to stop using it, and tick it again to turn it back on. You will usually be
asked for your password.
</para>

<para>
Adding repositories isn't offered, because PackageKit has no general way to do it. On Debian, add
them with <command>apt</command> or by adding a file to
<filename class="directory">/etc/apt/sources.list.d/</filename>.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="install-file">
<title>Installing a package file</title>

<para>
Open a <filename>.deb</filename> file from &konqueror;, or run:
</para>
<screen><userinput><command>tdepackagekit</command> <option>--install-file</option> <replaceable>./some-package.deb</replaceable></userinput></screen>
<para>
You see what will change, and a warning that local files can't be verified.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="notifier">
<title>The update notifier</title>

<para>
The update notifier, <command>tdepackagekit-notifier</command>, starts when you log in and puts an
icon in the system tray. Its tooltip says whether updates are available. When there are updates it
shows a short message, and the icon changes to a warning if any of them fix security problems.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The notifier's message</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="notifier-popup.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The notifier's message</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Click the icon to open the Updates page. Right-click it to check now, change settings, or quit
the notifier until the next login.
</para>

<para>
It waits about two minutes after login before its first check, so that logging in stays fast.
It only tells you about updates. <emphasis>It never installs anything by itself.</emphasis>
</para>

<para>
If you don't want an icon while there is nothing to install, turn on
<guilabel>Hide the tray icon when there are no updates</guilabel> in the settings. The notifier
then keeps running, and the icon only appears when updates are available.
</para>
</chapter>

<chapter id="settings">
<title>Settings</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The settings dialog</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="settings.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The settings dialog</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Open <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure Software
Management...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>, or <guimenuitem>Configure...</guimenuitem> in the
notifier's menu.
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Run the update notifier in the system tray</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Turns the notifier on or off. Turning it on starts it straight away, and it also
starts at every login. Turning it off stops it.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Hide the tray icon when there are no updates</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Keeps the tray clear while your software is up to date.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Check for updates</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How often the notifier downloads fresh package information: every 6 hours, daily,
weekly, monthly or never. With <guilabel>Never</guilabel> it still notices updates that another
tool has downloaded.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Notify Me About</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>All updates, or security updates only.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
The settings are stored in <filename>~/.trinity/share/config/tdepackagekitrc</filename>.
</para>
</chapter>

<chapter id="troubleshooting">
<title>Troubleshooting</title>

<sect1 id="errors">
<title>Error messages</title>

<para>
When something goes wrong, a message says what happened. Press <guibutton>Details</guibutton> to
see the full report from the package system, for example the output of
<command>dpkg</command>. The window can be resized or maximized to read long reports, and it
remembers the size you choose. You can select and copy the text, to search for it or include it
in a bug report.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>An error with details</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="error-dialog.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>An error with details</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="problems">
<title>Common problems</title>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><quote>PackageKit Not Available</quote></term>
<listitem><para>The PackageKit service could not be reached. Check that the
<literal>packagekit</literal> package is installed. The service starts on demand, so it doesn't
need to be running beforehand.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><quote>Not authorized</quote>, with no password prompt</term>
<listitem><para>No authentication agent is running. Install <literal>polkit-agent-tde</literal>
and log in again.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>The package system is busy</term>
<listitem><para>Another program, such as <command>apt</command> or an automatic update, is
changing packages. Wait for it to finish and try again.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>No icon in the system tray</term>
<listitem><para>Check that <guilabel>Run the update notifier in the system tray</guilabel> is on,
and that <guilabel>Hide the tray icon when there are no updates</guilabel> is off. To see whether
the notifier is running, run <userinput><command>pgrep -a
tdepackagekit-notifier</command></userinput>. You can start it by hand with
<userinput><command>tdepackagekit-notifier</command></userinput>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term>No application icons in the lists</term>
<listitem><para>Install <literal>apt-config-icons</literal> and refresh the package information.
See <xref linkend="app-icons"/>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="commands">
<title>Command line</title>

<para>
<command>tdepackagekit</command> takes these options, as well as the usual Trinity and TQt
options:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--updates</option></term>
<listitem><para>Open the Updates page.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--settings</option></term>
<listitem><para>Open the settings dialog.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--install-file</option> <replaceable>file</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Install a package file. It can be given more than once.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
Only one copy of &kappname; runs at a time. If it is already open, these options act on the open
window.
</para>

<para>
See also the manual pages: <userinput><command>man tdepackagekit</command></userinput> and
<userinput><command>man tdepackagekit-notifier</command></userinput>.
</para>
</chapter>

